  "description": "[Iliana Iotova](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova) became [Bulgaria's first female president](https://www.bta.bg/en/news/bulgaria/1049637-iliana-iotova-becomes-bulgaria-s-acting-president-after-constitutional-court-rul) on January 23, 2026 after Rumen Radev resigned. Radev would months later form the Progressive Bulgaria party, win parliamentary elections and [become Prime Minister](https://www.politico.eu/article/rumen-radev-sweeps-into-power-bulgaria-names-new-prime-minister/).\n\nBulgaria's next presidential elections are scheduled for October 2026. [A poll](https://m.novinite.com/articles/239845/Poll+Puts+Iliana+Yotova+Ahead+in+Bulgaria's+Presidential+Race) conducted in late June and early July 2026 showed \"around half of respondents said they would be likely to support her\". Then on July 24, 2026, [Iotova announced](https://www.bta.bg/en/news/bulgaria/1173487-head-of-state-iliana-iotova-to-run-for-president-in-upcoming-election) her intention to run for presidency because she believes she has \"the support of many people in Bulgaria\". President Iotova has [the highest approval rating](https://www.ibnaeu.com/en/2026/08/07/rumen-radev-first-100-days-government-bulgaria-poll/) among Bulgaria's senior state officials.&#x20;\n\nIotova served as the country's Vice President from January 2017 until her ascent to presidency.",
  "resolution_criteria": "This question will resolve as the percentage vote share received by Iliana Iotova in the first round of the Bulgarian presidential election scheduled to be held on October 25, 2026, as declared by the [Bulgarian Central Election Commission](https://results.cik.bg/).",
  "fine_print": "If the election is postponed or cancelled such that no result is declared on or before December 31, 2026, this question will be **annulled**.",
